<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caerleon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Isca Augusta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Caerleon is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-usk">River Usk</a> near Newport in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Around 75 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of Isca Augusta there as the base of the Second Legion Augusta, whose amphitheatre, baths and barracks survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a> later made Caerleon one of King Arthur's courts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caerleon.jpg" alt="Caerleon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marky-Son · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caerleon_vue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Caerleon, in Welsh Caerllion, lies in the Vale of Usk, about 5 miles (8 km) north-east of Newport city centre, and is now a community within Newport. The area has important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> sites. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it was home to the Silures and seems to have been the centre of a wealthy trading network. From the 5th century BCE a hillfort at Lodge Wood Camp, defended by three lines of ramparts and ditches and the largest fortified enclosure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a>, crowned the hill above the river. An excavation in 2000 found it occupied continuously until the Roman fortress was built. There is no evidence that it was taken militarily, and its abandonment may have been part of the terms of peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Caerleon was the headquarters of the Second Legion Augusta from about 75 to 300 CE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> called the place Isca after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-usk">River Usk</a>, and the name Caerleon may derive from the Welsh for fortress of the legion. The military amphitheatre, baths and barracks have been excavated, and a Roman harbour was found in 2011.</p><p> In the Middle Ages Caerleon was an administrative and religious centre of the Kingdom of Gwent and an early see associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubricius">Saint Dubricius</a>. After a Norman castle was built, the Welsh lords and the Normans contested the town. In 1171 Iorwerth ab Owain destroyed it and burned the castle, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a> seized and rebuilt in stone in 1217. Rhys Gethin took it in 1402 during the Glyndŵr Rising.</p><p>In his Historia Regum Britanniae, about 1136, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a> made Caerleon a major centre of British history, naming Belinus as its founder and describing it as the place of Arthur's coronation feast. Early Welsh poetry and Geoffrey named it as Arthur's capital, and the amphitheatre has been linked with the Round Table, although it shows no sign of sixth-century occupation. Gerald of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> wrote in 1191 that the Roman ambassadors had been received at Arthur's court there. Until the docks at Newport were built, Caerleon was the major port on the Usk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The remains of Isca Augusta, the National Roman Legion Museum and the Roman Baths Museum draw visitors and school parties, and a heritage trail marks the town. Caerleon has 86 listed buildings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cadoc">Saint Cadoc</a>'s Church and the Round Tower. An arts festival has been held each July since 2003, together with a Roman military display in the amphitheatre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Caerleon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the late 1st century three legions remained, at <b>Caerleon</b>, Chester and York, with auxiliary forts across Wales and the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Julius Frontinus finally subdued them around 74 to 78 and founded the legionary fortress of Isca at <b>Caerleon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/silures">Silures</a></li></ul></section>
